# Diff Review

{{/* why: one same-model plus one other-model review caught 56.7% of planted
errors, two same-model reviews 42.7% (Song, arXiv 2610.01471, 2026-10-06).
Greptile routes review to the other lab for the same reason (2026-07-21).
Theo's sol-loop and council use another provider. The user asked "did codex
review this?" in 4 sessions. */ -}}
## Reviewer

Use one reviewer from the other provider, on its newest top model: Codex
`gpt-6.2-astra` for code that Claude wrote, Claude `claude-opus-5-5` for code
that Codex wrote. If the orchestrator lists a newer top model for that
provider, use it and say which. If that provider is not available, say that
the review is not done. Do not use a different provider, and do not start a
second reviewer unless the user asks.

{{/* why: every source agrees the reviewer must not see the writer's
reasoning (Anthropic, Theo, Matt Pocock, poteto). Severity levels and "flag
code the request does not need" are from sol-loop; the second also covers
over-built code, the job of the simplify-code skill. "No findings is a
valid result" is from poteto's interrogate skill and the Codex adversarial
prompt. */ -}}
## Brief

Give the reviewer the request, the base commit, the diff command and the check
results. Do not give it your reasoning. Ask it to:

- Find bugs, broken callers, and places where the code does not do what was
  asked. Skip style unless it hides a bug.
- Flag code, tests or guards that the request does not need.
{{/* why: the user's test audit list (2026-11-19) cut to what one diff can
show. The two-edit test is Lauren Tan's (poteto) "test behavior, not
implementation" principle, installed as
pstack-principle-test-behavior-not-implementation. */ -}}
- Flag tests that still pass when the behavior breaks, or fail when only the
  internals change. For example: tests that mirror their mocks, check text,
  styling or page structure, test a library instead of our code, repeat
  another test, are skipped, or need retries to pass.
- Flag missing tests. A fix for a bug users hit needs a test that fails
  without the fix. A data migration needs a test of old data against new
  code. Code that must give the same result after a round trip or a second
  run needs a property test. A changed main user flow needs an end-to-end
  test, if the repository has them.
{{/* why: review rounds kept adding tests and guards for "synthetic edge
cases with no effect on real files" (skill-studio #551); the user: "lets not
worry about things that might happen in 0.001 percent of instances"
(2026-11-21). */ -}}
- Do not report rare cases, and do not ask for tests of them. A case is
  rare only if a normal user would not hit it within a month of everyday use
  and it would not lose data, cost more than about a dollar, expose a secret or leave the user
  stuck. No network, an app killed in the background, an expired sign-in, a
  first launch and a new day, week or month are never rare. Report a case
  with the normal use that reaches it.
{{/* why: the core of poteto's Comment Sicko (pstack-no-comments), which
the user runs by hand. Its keep list is shortened here. Rated nit so that
comments get fixed but never add a review round. */ -}}
- Flag comments that repeat the code, commented-out code, and comments that
  excuse a workaround. Keep licence headers, public API docs, and notes on
  behavior forced by something outside our code. Rate these `nit`.
- Rate each finding: `blocker` (the asked-for case is wrong), `should` (a real
  bug that users will hit, or a missing test from the list above), or `nit`
  (the rest).
- Say "no findings" when there are none.

{{/* why: #16 (2026-11-20) added a UI review hook and this step, because
cross-provider code review does not check spacing, motion or focus. */ -}}
When the diff changes UI files (`.tsx`, `.jsx`, `.css`, `.vue`, `.svelte`) and
the `emil-ui-review` skill is installed, also run it on those files. Read
`~/.agents/skills/emil-review-animations/SKILL.md` too when motion changed.

{{/* why: about one finding in four from a second-provider reviewer was
wrong (paddo.dev, 2026-10-27). "Do not agree just to end the review" is from
sol-loop. */ -}}
## Findings

Check each finding against the code before you fix it. Fix the real ones with
the smallest change. Reply to wrong ones with the reason. Do not agree with a
finding just to end the review.

{{/* why: the user does not read code, so something must remove code nobody
needs. The old automatic simplify pass ran on every change: 36 of 64 clear
runs changed code, 28 found nothing, about 11.5 minutes each (upper bound).
Running it only when the reviewer flags unneeded code skips the empty runs.
simplify-code checks callers and tests before it deletes, and reruns the
checks (user decision, 2026-11-19). */ -}}
If the reviewer flags code that the request does not need, read
`~/.agents/skills/simplify-code/SKILL.md` and run it on the diff before the
next round.

{{/* why: the old limit was two rounds. No source uses two. Anthropic's Claude
Code team and sol-loop use three; before Anthropic's cap, one PR went through
more than 50 rounds and later rounds "found a few more old ones each round"
(claude.dev, 2026-10-15). Here, before any limit, 24 of 139 changes had 3+
reviews and one had 21. Five rounds for risky areas and "out of rounds is not
approval" are from sol-loop. */ -}}
## When to stop

- Stop when a round has no `blocker` or `should` findings.
- Otherwise stop after 3 rounds, or 5 for security, access, money, data loss
  or concurrency. After round 1, the reviewer checks only the changed code and
  the open findings.
- A push that only merges the base branch is not a new round.
- If findings are still open at the end, give them to the user. Running out of
  rounds is not approval.
